<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monpazier</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monpazier is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, founded as a bastide in 1284 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> as duke of Aquitaine. Its regular grid of streets around an arcaded central square survives almost intact, and it is often cited as the best-preserved medieval planned new town in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monpazier.jpg" alt="Monpazier" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manfred Heyde · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monpazier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
